#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290493 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42c8fa6e-ec22-57a4-ace9-9ab79eb86dfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288485} {:answer/id #uuid "b84d2b62-4353-5f5a-a5c9-69ea117204f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289251} {:answer/id #uuid "d5049764-b1b5-5357-be51-767024d656d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289448} {:answer/id #uuid "dbf67f99-cbea-58b8-a183-8ed451369ef4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289496} {:answer/id #uuid "dd4a724f-68fe-5924-b70a-bdc7366c3c49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289506}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289448} :answer-field/id #uuid "1dc69ae9-136f-5a66-9a05-68c04a2bca60" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289806}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "487960be-d7be-5dc4-ae5e-e966e5d0db00" :question/math-academy-id "q-237022" :question/problem "Let $p=⟨3,5,x⟩$ and $q=⟨-3,1,2⟩.$ Given that the scalar projection of $p$ onto $q$ is zero, find the value of $x.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $p$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $q$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\np⊥q\\,⟹\\,p\\cdot q=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & ={p}_{x}\\cdot {q}_{x}+{p}_{y}\\cdot {q}_{y}+{p}_{z}\\cdot {q}_{z} \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-3)+5\\cdot 1+x\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2x-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & =0 \\\\ 2x-4 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =4 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290531 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c795865-da64-5e14-9024-3c7c02d718c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{5}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}(\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288124} {:answer/id #uuid "51fe0db3-d702-5875-904d-f79bd58f19bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{5}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}(x-1)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288600} {:answer/id #uuid "6e5a066a-1db0-5b07-b438-285b415f19bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{5}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}(x-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288802} {:answer/id #uuid "9611c013-b6fd-5217-9d39-883aca3329d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{5}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}(1-\\frac{x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289037} {:answer/id #uuid "c91e7928-ce32-56f4-8b9f-43257961903a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{5}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}(x+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289366}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288802} :answer-field/id #uuid "94c0f155-a00b-507b-a0ba-cb1120435b6e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290061}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "00c040ca-17ee-59e4-9387-a983daa9bae0" :question/math-academy-id "q-2420" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int x{5}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\displaystyle \\int x{5}^{x}\\text{d}x$ as $\\displaystyle \\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}={5}^{x}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\frac{{5}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int x\\cdot {5}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\cdot \\frac{{5}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}-\\int \\frac{{5}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{5}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{x{5}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}-\\int \\frac{{5}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{x{5}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}-\\frac{{5}^{x}}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5))^{2}}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{5}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}(x-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5})+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292788 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ef421630-f5d7-503b-a51d-c030fb68f55b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\operatorname{cos}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291633}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291633} :answer-field/id #uuid "094528fc-3453-5536-a752-6b339b2db0f4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291770} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "596a7662-dda2-51ec-baec-f535e64f4466" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{sin}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291012}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291012} :answer-field/id #uuid "c71cfe73-e3d4-5f45-befa-e936a466f3bf" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292341}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "bfcc6f55-69b8-5619-9a26-74c52bdc8a72" :question/math-academy-id "q-274855" :question/problem "![](images/d9/d906e74fdd78e074ab410d1042c134c892fd1b50b78b0f50a76ccf2ee203c203.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal radius $6$ and vertical radius $4,$ as shown above. Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ that traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(6,0).$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}$,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(a,0).$\n\nIn our case, $a=6$ and $b=4.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292790 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc69e957-84d8-51fb-b9af-1954bdce66ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{sin}\\theta -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291446}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291446} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c1ea5ae-79aa-5e96-b4b7-7d8848392eac" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292065} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34610a7c-3778-500e-9c93-c897614fb8c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290846}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290846} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0bd58a5-ce48-5141-ab31-4b44a565ed83" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292366}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "7cca84a9-8a4f-5701-ae4a-5497d9f10edd" :question/math-academy-id "q-274862" :question/problem "![](images/c9/c9340314acbaab9f4b4e9180e3028848ad80e8fa33e26bff32eaf47b00d164a2.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at $(2,-1)$ with horizontal radius $5$ and vertical radius $3,$ as shown above. Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ that traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(7,-1).$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}$,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(h+a,k).$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(2,-1),$ $a=5$ and $b=3.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292791 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c83eb61b-d093-55e1-8524-2613dce4c86e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291491}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291491} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3def395-9142-57f7-80c2-7ffaf2820a72" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292496} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0540bab6-0f5a-5dbc-8773-de1617adc993" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290661}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290661} :answer-field/id #uuid "fcee20d9-5ee6-569d-8ebf-8c9c64747a52" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292518}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f43b2664-c814-5c17-82ea-86e156e4166f" :question/math-academy-id "q-274866" :question/problem "![](images/06/061c7c34639d88b72d1a3045e794e2aa149b3331dafa33447071aed80b861088.png)\n\nThe quarter-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$ Assume $\\theta $ is between $0$ and $2\\pi .$\n\n$\\theta \\in [${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$]$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(3,1),$ $a=2$ and $b=3.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the ellipse and the line parallel to the $x$-axis and crossing the center of the ellipse.\n\n![](images/6d/6dc795d39f90b6df177fe815a9135e7eadbe0b0d7d01214009de495e704486f7.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$, we get the quarter-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292813 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ba6a314-9690-51f8-ac65-d87aa7adaabd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291309}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291309} :answer-field/id #uuid "de32afac-e614-53fa-b799-24f12769f89d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292428}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "fc161987-10e4-5ee6-a6c6-ed110201a1f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-275220" :question/problem "Calculate the area $A$ of the parallelogram spanned by $a=⟨-4,-2,1⟩$ and $b=⟨0,-2,-1⟩.$\n\n$A=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -4 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-2)\\cdot (-1)-(-2)\\cdot 1)i-((-4)\\cdot (-1)-0\\cdot 1)j+((-4)\\cdot (-2)-0\\cdot (-2))k \\\\  & =4i-4j+8k \\\\  & =⟨4,-4,8⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the corresponding parallelogram is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\,a\\times b\\,| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+{8}^{2}} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292814 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff2a44dc-fdc6-559d-9985-a9e6ce7b18ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291722}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291722} :answer-field/id #uuid "60989695-b200-54ed-9968-72677234d5ec" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292028}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1c7348e7-1921-5e09-8018-775e68160619" :question/math-academy-id "q-275222" :question/problem "Calculate the area ${A}_{△}$ of the triangle spanned by $a=⟨-2,-1,0⟩$ and $b=⟨-4,2,-2⟩.$\n\n${A}_{△}=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 2 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-1)\\cdot (-2)-2\\cdot 0)i-((-2)\\cdot (-2)-(-4)\\cdot 0)j+((-2)\\cdot 2-(-4)\\cdot (-1))k \\\\  & =2i-4j-8k \\\\  & =⟨2,-4,-8⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the corresponding parallelogram is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\,a\\times b\\,| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+(-8)^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{21}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of the triangle is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{2\\sqrt{21}}{2}=\\sqrt{21}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292815 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47d3a2b5-466f-590c-8ea8-479084264b69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{34}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290925}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290925} :answer-field/id #uuid "45ec0f2b-c1b4-5c37-a450-1b90d5f71f78" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291960}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "41352b96-198e-5a5d-978c-31702eac06ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-275224" :question/problem "Calculate the area ${A}_{△}$ of the $△\\,ABC$, if $A(-2,0,-1)$, $B(-3,3,-4)$, $C(0,-2,2).$\n\n${A}_{△}=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(-3i+3j-4k)-(-2i-k) \\\\  & =-i+3j-3k \\\\  & =⟨-1,3,-3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(-2j+2k)-(-2i-k) \\\\  & =2i-2j+3k \\\\  & =⟨2,-2,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(3\\cdot 3-(-2)\\cdot (-3))i-((-1)\\cdot 3-2\\cdot (-3))j+((-1)\\cdot (-2)-2\\cdot 3)k \\\\  & =3i-3j-4k \\\\  & =⟨3,-3,-4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(-3)^{2}+(-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{34}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{34}}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292816 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42aac3c5-ad83-55c9-bc67-806f2c57a155" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{22}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290908}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290908} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee54665e-3213-5a19-9284-f386f859abe3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292475}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "a1a70e2a-5e7c-521f-a8eb-605197f729a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-275225" :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $CH$ of the $△\\,ABC$, if $A(-2,0,-1)$, $B(0,-2,-1)$, $C(-3,-2,0).$\n\n$CH=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $CH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,ABC$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB\n$$\n\nSo, to find $CH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(-2j-k)-(-2i-k) \\\\  & =2i-2j \\\\  & =⟨2,-2,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(-3i-2j)-(-2i-k) \\\\  & =-i-2j+k \\\\  & =⟨-1,-2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-2)\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot 0)i-(2\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot 0)j+(2\\cdot (-2)-(-1)\\cdot (-2))k \\\\  & =-2i-2j-6k \\\\  & =⟨-2,-2,-6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(-2)^{2}+(-6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{44} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{2\\sqrt{11}}{2}=\\sqrt{11}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $AB$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB \\\\ \\sqrt{11} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{11} & =CH\\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{\\sqrt{22}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292825 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e298e5d-8e02-5292-b786-b6d2de741151" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290966}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290966} :answer-field/id #uuid "0bc7b170-a232-59eb-8177-5c58c013f18d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291777}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ca4288cb-fc7d-55d7-abec-0f51a4050e6e" :question/math-academy-id "q-275243" :question/problem "Suppose $|\\,c\\,|=4$ and the angle between vectors $c$ and $d$ is $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Find the scalar projection of $c$ onto $d.$\n\n${\\text{comp}}_{d}\\,c=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{d}\\,c & =|\\,c\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292826 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43038c56-d384-5570-86af-704e0880166f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{13}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290909}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290909} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d92e0bb-bf02-560d-8309-29d74166634f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292073}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5b790374-4bad-5824-af22-323909fd339e" :question/math-academy-id "q-275244" :question/problem "Let $p=⟨2,1,-2⟩$ and $q=⟨3,2,0⟩.$ Find the exact value of the scalar projection of $p$ onto $q.$\n\n${\\text{comp}}_{q}\\,p=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{q}\\,p & =\\frac{p\\cdot q}{|\\,q\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{p}_{x}{q}_{x}+{p}_{y}{q}_{y}+{p}_{z}{q}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{q}_{x}^{2}+{q}_{y}^{2}+{q}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 3+1\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot 0}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{13}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292827 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7c90fa25-691f-58a4-b734-448a6fca3732" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291159}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291159} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c398229-da8b-56ee-b803-4aa60968cde1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291938}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "911cdd8c-0aad-54d0-80d8-17350e5cfd46" :question/math-academy-id "q-275245" :question/problem "Let $c=⟨1,2,-3⟩$ and $d=⟨2,1,2⟩.$ Find the scalar projection of $c$ onto $d.$\n\n${\\text{comp}}_{d}\\,c=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{d}\\,c & =\\frac{c\\cdot d}{|\\,d\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{c}_{x}{d}_{x}+{c}_{y}{d}_{y}+{c}_{z}{d}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{d}_{x}^{2}+{d}_{y}^{2}+{d}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 2+2\\cdot 1+(-3)\\cdot 2}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{9}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292828 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d7f3d04-9d9a-5db3-8121-2770a9e7e2ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291102}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291102} :answer-field/id #uuid "35278910-7191-599c-8899-b91af97d2d88" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291910}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ec0f207d-cef1-5071-a74a-2301f10fe8e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-275247" :question/problem "Let $c=⟨4,-3,0⟩$ and $d=⟨2,1,-2⟩.$ Find, ${\\text{comp}}_{d}\\,c-{\\text{comp}}_{c}\\,d.$\n\n${\\text{comp}}_{d}\\,c-{\\text{comp}}_{c}\\,d=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{d}\\,c & =\\frac{c\\cdot d}{|\\,d\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{c}_{x}{d}_{x}+{c}_{y}{d}_{y}+{c}_{z}{d}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{d}_{x}^{2}+{d}_{y}^{2}+{d}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot 2+(-3)\\cdot 1+0\\cdot (-2)}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{9}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{c}\\,d & =\\frac{c\\cdot d}{|\\,c\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{c}_{x}{d}_{x}+{c}_{y}{d}_{y}+{c}_{z}{d}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{c}_{x}^{2}+{c}_{y}^{2}+{c}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+(-3)^{2}+{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{25}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{5} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{d}\\,c-{\\text{comp}}_{c}\\,d & =\\frac{5}{3}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292829 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "914d3440-61a2-5cbd-915b-d851c90abbd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291255}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291255} :answer-field/id #uuid "da2ac539-adda-5fda-b798-5c65309f7150" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292405}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0e5cdb13-4156-58cf-ae33-5a314b89945e" :question/math-academy-id "q-275253" :question/problem "Let $p=⟨-1,2,1⟩$ and $q=⟨2,x,0⟩.$ Given that the scalar projection of $p$ onto $q$ is zero, find the value of $x.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since $p$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $q$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\np⊥q\\,⟹\\,p\\cdot q=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & ={p}_{x}\\cdot {q}_{x}+{p}_{y}\\cdot {q}_{y}+{p}_{z}\\cdot {q}_{z} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 2+2\\cdot x+1\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =-2+2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & =0 \\\\ -2+2x & =0 \\\\ 2x & =2 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292830 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0eb0079d-45e8-5b05-b410-c68104c01c2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290701}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290701} :answer-field/id #uuid "3af26996-2272-545c-90e4-e37f7e8325a7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291933}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0b211c2b-bc36-53db-aca9-0fadea78784c" :question/math-academy-id "q-275256" :question/problem "Let $a=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ x \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Given that the scalar projection of $a$ onto $b$ is $0,$ find the value of $x.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since $p$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $q$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\np⊥q\\,⟹\\,p\\cdot q=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & ={p}_{x}\\cdot {q}_{x}+{p}_{y}\\cdot {q}_{y}+{p}_{z}\\cdot {q}_{z} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 0+2\\cdot x+4\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2x+8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & =0 \\\\ 2x+8 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =-8 \\\\ x & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292868 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c438cd2-2f9a-56e9-ba78-198237723b47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290809}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290809} :answer-field/id #uuid "9902feec-4c1f-5bad-9074-0772c0883e74" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292218}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ff3bf305-a423-59b5-a761-0e3f9822d595" :question/math-academy-id "q-275585" :question/problem "If $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 7 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -3\\end{bmatrix},$ then $det(A)=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}-5 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 7 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -3\\end{vmatrix} & =-5\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 5 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{vmatrix}-3\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{vmatrix}+0\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 7 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-5(-21-(-10))-3(-3-0)+0 \\\\  & =-5\\cdot (-11)-3\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =55+9 \\\\  & =64\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294935 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "92fade62-72f5-5c55-aa78-c2d0dbaf2b44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{49}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293509}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293509} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9711613-beb2-54c9-ab19-cae6c7783280" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294539}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5ee547bf-f7e1-51f3-bed2-e58fc1c418f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-277921" :question/problem "![](images/ab/ab3d1fefcefa12133ec4ea7e3e6ce744c88f5a9b25029c950254706097cb4c4d.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?\n\n{{field-1}}$=1$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=2$ and the vertical radius has length $b=7.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{7}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{49} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294936 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "177cbba2-b79a-5f5d-9c48-f99cc18456dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293025}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293025} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b444896-e044-5921-b7f7-0da6232a651c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294016} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89cbf232-7d5e-5370-98f0-434b0ca4424c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293468}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293468} :answer-field/id #uuid "643cdb73-3055-524d-9fe7-30a337996809" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294284}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5b7e4e8f-cd81-5ee3-ac55-a56d0433ac6d" :question/math-academy-id "q-277923" :question/problem "For the ellipse \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{64}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{81}=1,\n$$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=64$ and ${b}^{2}=81.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=64,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{64}=8.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=81,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{81}=9.$\n\nThus, the semi-major axis has length $b=9,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $a=8.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 9=18,$ and the minor axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 8=16.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295041 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "001a0d9f-37c7-5af8-8262-d33442935b95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{64}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292939}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292939} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0c14e4e-9192-5fc5-bf7f-b01bc1b02f87" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294511}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "79f5cd4e-9f15-597c-8503-3bd19ab1ee0f" :question/math-academy-id "q-279101" :question/problem "![](images/eb/eb9c9b61de232ab6c190a3a324ff3ee2515c61f72a16e2814d77f94a60255501.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?\n\n{{field-1}}$=1$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=8$ and the vertical radius has length $b=6.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{8}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{6}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{64}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295042 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0845d678-553d-5f9b-8f0c-cc3b9b0a3bcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{64}+\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{81}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292966}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292966} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e7e8acb-7222-57bd-8d98-c0f67679b115" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294501}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "40ba0575-d790-5658-a955-78c24f417888" :question/math-academy-id "q-279125" :question/problem "![](images/e1/e1bfe4335b41f14dce0f3d614ca108a4d6d83c4aa65fc44f169431175c82f1b5.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?\n\n{{field-1}}$=1$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=8$ and the vertical radius has length $b=\\frac{9}{2}.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{8}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{\\left(\\frac{9}{2}\\right)}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{64}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{\\left(\\frac{81}{4}\\right)} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{64}+\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{81} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295043 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dea75317-4676-5892-9874-67ea7ff4cdf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{121}+\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293799}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293799} :answer-field/id #uuid "b698fac5-48ad-5a4b-97a3-0c37682df74c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294584}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "087f317c-361a-5e32-80c0-0140a69715d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-279126" :question/problem "![](images/41/410a33acd13bc05e65a8f11defb9828a68bf5b80826305adefe2d008afa245c4.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?\n\n{{field-1}}$=1$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=\\frac{11}{2}$ and the vertical radius has length $b=\\frac{3}{2}.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{\\left(\\frac{11}{2}\\right)}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\left(\\frac{121}{4}\\right)}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{\\left(\\frac{9}{4}\\right)} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{4{x}^{2}}{121}+\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{9} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295044 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eb563e16-bedd-5fd3-b345-0e1ce12a034a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{289}+\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{49}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293850}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293850} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a79c72b-8065-5c15-9144-a0efaabaec31" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294422}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "eb9dc1b1-fc7f-54d8-8fe7-138d381a4ada" :question/math-academy-id "q-279130" :question/problem "![](images/1f/1fcd12762c7b86e1d40813a747b75e1f9b21d83787824f5f48725ac9a1dddff6.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?\n\n{{field-1}}$=1$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=\\frac{17}{2}$ and the vertical radius has length $b=\\frac{7}{2}.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{\\left(\\frac{17}{2}\\right)}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\left(\\frac{289}{4}\\right)}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{\\left(\\frac{49}{4}\\right)} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{4{x}^{2}}{289}+\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{49} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295045 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e63fb47-9980-5e87-8d24-ce7eca9acd5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{49}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293392}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293392} :answer-field/id #uuid "48bfec57-cd37-5bd5-90c5-e5ddcac17dea" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294186}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "456e704c-c6f8-5c90-abc7-50e898dff618" :question/math-academy-id "q-279151" :question/problem "![](images/0f/0f3972825271a2f9471657919ed3669c63e03cd3740a436ab14cc30ed65b73bc.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?\n\n{{field-1}}$=1$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=\\frac{7}{2}$ and the vertical radius has length $b=4.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\left(\\frac{49}{4}\\right)}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{4{x}^{2}}{49}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295046 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b57ce2a3-4e47-5f3d-b3c5-b2a4d2ffbc87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{81}+\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{169}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293641}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293641} :answer-field/id #uuid "6260a0b3-57b4-59ba-b250-89d695b2465a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294280}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "09a0ffab-31a1-59c6-932b-05d507e393c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-279158" :question/problem "![](images/c5/c583eabd1719e94dc727b876b944c839a39124adb341d88357cf954dbed79646.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?\n\n{{field-1}}$=1$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=\\frac{9}{2}$ and the vertical radius has length $b=\\frac{13}{2}.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{\\left(\\frac{9}{2}\\right)}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{\\left(\\frac{13}{2}\\right)}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\left(\\frac{81}{4}\\right)}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{\\left(\\frac{169}{4}\\right)} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{4{x}^{2}}{81}+\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{169} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295047 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6dc25ece-e5e8-5341-b369-60c89d7b7cf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293391}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293391} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b5ea0c3-f3db-587a-a55c-d246902036fa" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294196} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8edd